/*
 * The AI's move scoring tables: what each module adds to or takes off a
 * move's score, and under what condition.
 *
 * Every move starts at a base score and each module the trainer has flagged
 * runs independently, walking its own `groups` in order. A group is one rule:
 * `when` (one or more condition indices) leading to `outcome` (`score`,
 * optional `chance`, `terminate`), with an optional `gate` chance wrapping it.
 *
 * A group carrying more conditions than the one before it is **opening a
 * nested block**: the extra leading conditions become guards that every later
 * group must also satisfy, until an `Otherwise` group closes the level. This
 * is the structure the source marks with blank lines, and it matters - flatten
 * Thunder Wave's Volt Absorb block and its inner branches fire against targets
 * that have no Volt Absorb at all.
 *
 * `chance` is the game's own numerator over 256; the percentage the source
 * prints is a rounding of that. `terminate` stops this module only - the
 * others carry on, which is what makes them independent.
 *
 * `when` is an index into MOVE_SCORING_CONDITIONS rather than a string.
 * That array is also the evaluator's list of work: one predicate per entry,
 * and an index with no predicate behind it is a condition the planner cannot
 * answer and should say so about rather than guess.
 *
 * MOVE_SCORING_FLAGS maps each module onto the per-trainer flag in
 * trainer_flags.js that switches it on.
 *
 * Generated from https://bparkpk.github.io/PKMoveScoring/ and hand-checked.
 * Regenerate with tools/gen-move-scoring.js.
 *
 * Written without indentation on purpose: this is a large table and the
 * whitespace cost three times the data. Read it through the generator's
 * report, or the site, rather than by scrolling it.
 */
